// Between Frontiers: armed forces in peace (phase 5, slice A). Contract:
// crates/bf-sim/src/war/state.rs (PeaceRules); reasoning in docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Forças em paz".
// Licence: CC BY-SA 4.0.
//
// Owner's decisions 1 and 2 (2026-11-17), kept swappable here so that a "no" costs little:
// - `soldiers: Forces` puts in the pops the men of the formations of
//   data/scenario/1925/forces.ron; `PopulationShare(n)` goes back to the old rule, n per mille
//   of the population as soldiers and the state's fixed basket of arms.
// - `peace_size: ShareOfServiceAge` keeps each country's men under arms at the share of its men
//   of service age it had in 1925, scaled by the `conscription` effect of the law in force over
//   the one of 1925 when `law_scaling` is on; `Fixed1925` keeps the 1925 numbers.
// - `share_override`: a country's share of its men of service age under arms, per mille, in
//   place of the one measured in 1925.
#![enable(implicit_some)]
(
    // The rule while the forces are off (`forces` in data/rules.ron, off until the PR that
    // switches them on, judged by the aggregates on its own; `BF_RULES=forces` or
    // `bf-bench --rule forces` turn them on for a run, `GameData::enable_forces` in tests).
    soldiers: PopulationShare(10),
    peace_size: ShareOfServiceAge,
    law_scaling: true,
    // Weeks to make good the equipment a formation lacks (design parameter).
    replace_weeks: 26,
    // Years for a force to move to a newer generation of equipment once the country makes it
    // (Industrial stage at home, or the technology diffused somewhere: bought abroad). Design
    // parameter: armies re-equip over a decade or so, unit by unit, not all at once.
    modernize_years: 10,
    // Multiplies the forces' consumption in peace (not the wear of their equipment). 1: the
    // catalogue as written. The rest of the military budget is spent anyway (equipment beyond
    // the needs), so a larger scale only moves money from that to training ammunition, which
    // the 1925 arms industry does not make enough of (docs/war.pt-BR.md, "Forças em paz").
    upkeep_scale: "1",
    share_override: [],
)
